// std::time: the time catalogue, and `Duration` and `Time` (GH #1076, U4).
//
// The two time types are declarations of the unit dialect, not types
// the compiler knows by heart: the units are a catalogue like any a
// program declares, a literal's suffix (`500ms`, `2min`) is one of
// these units, and the algebra that types a program's quantities and
// points types these two. A program's units join this catalogue only
// through an equation it writes against one of these (`unit tick =
// 10 ms;`).
//
// `Duration` and `Time` keep their representation: a value is a count
// of nanoseconds (a `Time`, since the epoch the runtime defines; the
// point has no `origin:` clause), a topic field of one is tagged `u`
// and `t` as it always was, and a `Duration` prints its nanoseconds
// (`1500000000ns`).
unit ns;
unit us = 1_000 ns;
unit ms = 1_000 us;
unit s = 1_000 ms;
unit min = 60 s;
unit h = 60 min;
unit day = 24 h;

/// A span of time, counted in nanoseconds.
type Duration = quantity Int in ns;

/// An instant, a count of nanoseconds since the epoch.
type Time = point Duration;
